84
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.500
I like to think of this as a financial
slider of risk.

85
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.995
Every reimbursement method shifts the
financial responsibility back and forth

86
00:04:30.995 --> 00:04:32.500
between you and the supplier.

87
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.975
And where you set that slider mostly
depends on how clear and mature your

88
00:04:36.975 --> 00:04:38.500
project's scope actually is.

89
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.000
Let's look at the two extreme ends of this
slider.

90
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.500
On one end, you have time and materials.

91
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.500
Here, you're paying per hour or per unit.

92
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:51.763
You, the client, hold all the financial
risk if things take longer than expected,

93
00:04:51.763 --> 00:04:54.500
which is kind of necessary when your scope
is vague.

94
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.500
But on the complete opposite end is fixed
price.

95
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.500
This shifts the overrun risk entirely onto
the supplier.

96
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.562
You get great predictability for a clearly
defined scope, though the supplier is

97
00:05:04.562 --> 00:05:07.500
probably going to charge you a premium to
take on that extra risk.

98
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.500
Now, sitting somewhere in the middle is
the cost plus method.

99
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.776
This is where you pay the actual, literal
cost of the work, plus a fixed fee or

100
00:05:14.776 --> 00:05:17.500
percentage on top for the supplier's
profit and overhead.

101
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.973
You're mostly going to use this when a
project is incredibly urgent and you

102
00:05:20.973 --> 00:05:24.025
literally just need to get boots on the
ground before the scope is even fully

103
00:05:24.025 --> 00:05:24.500
flushed out.

104
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.178
And hey, if you really want to force some
collaboration, there's the target cost

105
00:05:29.178 --> 00:05:29.500
method.

106
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.500
You both agree on a target price up front,
and then you work together to hit it.

107
00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.500
If you come in under budget, you share the
savings.

108
00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.500
But if things go over, you share the pain
at an agreed rate.

109
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:42.500
It's a fantastic way to align everyone's
financial motivations perfectly.

110
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.500
So to wrap up this section, remember.

111
00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:49.500
Picking your reimbursement method is not
just some boring admin task.

112
00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.500
It literally dictates who gets stuck
holding the bag if things go south.

113
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.489
From the client owning the risk in time
and materials, all the way to the supplier

114
00:05:58.489 --> 00:06:01.500
